          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Traditionally, a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+symmetry"} global
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetry"} symmetry
            is defined as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/invariance"} invariance
            (e.g. of a physical system) under a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+action"} group action
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mechanics"} classical mechanics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectomorphisms"} symplectomorphisms
            leaving a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian"} Hamiltonian
            invariant.
          %p
            However, many systems of interest depart, or
            = succeed "," do
              %strong generalize
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+field+theory"} classical field theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+system"} quantum system
            (or as both, as a
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertical+categorification"} vertical categorification
            of groups to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-groups"}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                \-groups
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+prequantum+geometry"} higher prequantum geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
            (or
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra"} algebra
            %strong generalized global symmetries
            is thought to be the appropriate notion of symmetry when both of these generalizations take place at the same time.
          %p#OriginOfTheTerminology
            The terminology
            %em generalized global symmetry
            for such a situation is due to
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#GaiottoKapustinSeibergWillett14"} Gaiotto, Kapustin, Seiberg &amp; Willett (2014)
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operators"} operators
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integrand"} integrand
            of a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral"} path integral
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/submanifolds"} submanifolds
            (of
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/codimension"} codimension
            %a{:href => "#GaiottoKapustinSeibergWillett14"} GKSW14
            propose to regard any quantum operator with the topological property as a symmetry of the quantum system even if they do not have inverses. It is in this sense that the concept of symmetry is regarded as being generalized.
          %p
            As emphasized by
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#FHTL09"} Freed, Hopkins, Teleman &amp; Lurie (2009)
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#FMT22"} Freed, Moore &amp; Teleman (2022)
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+algebra"} higher algebra
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "John+Elias+Roberts#Roberts79"} Roberts (1979)
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QFT+with+defects"} defects in QFT
            which have routinely been described via
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+algebra"} higher
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+algebra"} categorical algebra
            = succeed ";" do
              %a{:href => "#FRS04"} Fuchs, Runkel &amp; Schweigert (2004)
            = succeed ";" do
              %a{:href => "#FFRS07"} Fröhlich, Fuchs, Runkel &amp; Schweigert (2007)
            = succeed ";" do
              %a{:href => "QFT+with+defects#Lurie09"} Lurie (2009) §4.3
            = succeed ";" do
              %a{:href => "QFT+with+defects#DavydovRunkelKong11"} Davydov, Runkel &amp; Kong (2011)
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "QFT+with+defects#FuchsSchweigertValentino13"} Fuchs, Schweigert &amp; Valentino (2013)
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#FRS02I"} Fuchs, Runkel &amp; Schweigert (2002)
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#BT17"} Bhardwaj and Tachikawa (2017)
          %p The upshot is that just as groups correctly describe the symmetries of classical mechanics, higher algebra is thought to describe the “generalized global symmetries” of a quantum field theory.
          %p
            In this sense, the concept may be better understood as
            %strong higher quantum symmetries
            (even though the name “quantum symmetries” is already used in the Physics literature for a “dual” symmetry that arises after gauging a symmetry, originating in
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Vafa89"} Vafa (1989)
          %p
            A significant part of the current Physics literature on the topic restricts to the study of
            %em finite
            generalized symmetries, thus avoiding questions regarding the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth"} smoothness
            of the corresponding higher algebra (in contrast with the situation of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-group"} higher groups
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+infinity-groupoid"} smooth infinity-groupoid
            %em bare
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fusion+categories"} fusion category
            (and their vertical categorifications as in e.g.
            %a{:href => "#DY23"} Decoppet and Yu (2023)
            and
            %a{:href => "#BDSY24"} Bhardwaj et al. 2024
            based on
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#JF20nf"} Johnson-Freyd 2020
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%27t+Hooft+anomaly"} 't Hooft anomaly
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+quantum+field+theory"} topological quantum field theory
            defined through the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Turaev-Viro+model"} Turaev-Viro model
            and their would-be higher generalizations. Unfortunately, not only these expectations but even fundamental questions such as precisely
            %em how
            a smooth higher algebra acts on a quantum field theory (beyond the slogan of
            = succeed ")" do
              %em
                a symmetry operator inserted on a codimension
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
                submanifold acts on quantum operators supported on dimension
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d-1
                submanifolds
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+action"} group action
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantomorphism+group"} quantomorphism group
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+circle+n-bundle"} prequantum circle n-bundle
            becomes this after
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motivic+quantization"} motivic quantization
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+bundle"} vector bundles
            such as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-vector+bundle"} 2-vector bundles
            or, in much greater generality, an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-vector+bundle"} n-vector bundle
            over some
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E-%E2%88%9E+ring"} E-∞ ring
          %h2#freedmooreteleman_quiche
            Freed-Moore-Teleman
            %em quiche
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#FMT22"} Freed, Moore and Teleman (2022)
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+functorial+field+theory"} extended functorial field theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            defines a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+field+theory"} topological field theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Sym
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sym(T)
            defined by the generalized symmetries of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            %em Symmetry TFT
            and that, in turn,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            can be recovered from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Sym
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sym(T)
            via manipulations on its boundary.
          %p
            The main concept is that of a
            = succeed "," do
              %strong quiche
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi σ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ρ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\sigma,\rho)
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            is a fully extended
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n+1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ρ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho
            is a
            = succeed "-\u201Cmodule\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/domain+wall"} domain wall
            between TFT’s). Then the statement that an
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            has
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi σ
                  %mo =
                  %mi Sym
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma=Sym(T)
            as its symmetry theory consists of another pair
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mover
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %mo ,
                  %mi θ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\tilde{T},\theta)
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde{T}
            is a
            = succeed "-module" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi θ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta
            is an “isomorphism”
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi θ
                  %mo :
                  %mi ρ
                  %msub
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi σ
                  %mover
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\theta: \rho\otimes_{\sigma}\tilde{T} \cong T
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ρ
                  %msub
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi σ
                  %mover
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho\otimes_{\sigma}\tilde{T}
            is schematically understood as placing
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            on a spacetime
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mi n
                  %mo ×
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^n\times [0,1]
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ρ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho
            on one end and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde{T}
            on the other, such that collapsing the slab gives
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^n
          %p
            Some remarks about this proposal are in order. First, the kind of symmetries that are usually dealt with here are of finite character (related to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+with+finite+homotopy+groups"} homotopy type with finite homotopy groups
            as explained in Appendix A). This allows to avoid discussions regarding smoothness of categories. However, as highlighted in (
            %a{:href => "#KK23"}> Kang &amp; Kang (2023)
            ), overlooking this fact has led to some inaccurate conclusions obtained from applying techniques that in reality are valid only in the discrete setting but not in the smooth setting. Work aiming towards addressing this issue is in (
            %a{:href => "#GRT23"}> Gripaios, Randal-Williams, and Tooby-Smith (2023)
            ). Second, some statements (which are not necessarily minor points) are still only schematic. This includes the isomorphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi θ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta
            mentioned. This is partly due to the following assertion:
            = succeed "\u201C" do
              %em “Recall that the cobordism hypothesis enables a calculus of such functors (FTFT’s) in terms of duality data inside the codomain category C. Turning to nontopological theories, a similar calculus is not in place and is a subject of wide interest.
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#FMT22"} FMT22
            %a{:href => "#GradyPavlov21"} Grady and Pavlov (2021)
            on the
            %em geometric
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordism+hypothesis"} cobordism hypothesis
            has clear relevance to these two remarks.
